    "message": {
     "content": "### The Bottom Line: **Do Not Accept As-Is \u2014 Negotiate**\n\nYou are in a strong negotiating position: you are happy at Meta, were passively recruited, and hold an equivalent Senior/Staff-level role (IC6 $\\approx$ L6). \n\nGoogle\u2019s initial offer is **solid, but it is an opening bid with substantial room for improvement**. Leaving Meta without negotiating would leave tens of thousands of dollars (if not six figures) on the table.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Breakdown of the Offer vs. Meta\n\n| Component | Meta (IC6) | Google (L6 Offer) | Analysis |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **Slight decrease** (Range is $183k\u2013$271k) |\n| **Target Bonus** | 15% (~$36,000) | 20% ($47,000) | **+$11,000** |\n| **Total Target Cash** | $276,000 | $282,000 | **+$6,000** (essentially flat) |\n| **Equity (Annualized)** | ~$250,000/yr | $270,000/yr ($1.08M / 4) | **+$20,000/yr** (straight-line) |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | N/A | $0 | **$0** (typical first offer) |\n| **Average Total Comp** | **~$526,000** | **~$552,000** | **+$26,000/yr** (only ~5% increase) |\n\n---\n\n### 2. Critical Nuances You Must Factor In\n\n#### A. The Google Vesting Schedule \"Front-Loading\"\nUnlike Meta\u2019s quarterly even vest (25% per year), Google typically front-loads new-hire equity grants (historically **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** or **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%**):\n* **Year 1 Total Comp:** ~$638,000 to ~$692,000 (due to 33\u201338% vesting in Year 1).\n* **Year 4 Total Comp:** Drops sharply to ~$390,000\u2013$410,000 without stock growth and heavy refreshers.\n\n#### B. The \"Refresher Stacking\" Reality\nThe recruiter is correct that Google equity refreshers vest over multiple years and stack. However, **Meta does the exact same thing**, often with more predictable and generous equity refreshes. You cannot rely on hypothetical future refreshers to bridge the compensation gap in Years 3 and 4.\n\n#### C. The Base Salary\nThey offered you **$235,000**, which is lower than your current $240,000 Meta base and sitting near the midpoint of their $183k\u2013$271k band. You should never take a base pay cut when switching Big Tech companies horizontally.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to Ask For (Your Counter-Offer)\n\nTarget the upper quartile of the L6 PM band:\n\n1. **Sign-on Bonus: Ask for $75,000 \u2013 $100,000**\n   * *Rationale:* You are leaving behind $200,000+ in near-term vesting Meta RSUs and walking into a new review cycle. Google routinely uses sign-on bonuses to bridge this gap.\n2. **Equity (GSUs): Counter with $1,250,000 \u2013 $1,300,000 over 4 years**\n   * *Rationale:* $1.08M is standard for an initial offer, but L6 PM equity can reach $1.2M\u2013$1.35M+ for competitive hires.\n3. **Base Salary: Counter with $255,000**\n   * *Rationale:* Matches/beats your current $240,000 Meta base while staying comfortably within their posted $271,000 ceiling.\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Communicate the Counter\n\nCall or email your recruiter with a collaborative, appreciative, yet firm tone:\n\n> *\"I\u2019m excited about the team and the scope of the L6 role, and I\u2019ve really enjoyed getting to know everyone. However, compensation is an important consideration for me. Because I was not actively looking and am in a strong position at Meta\u2014making $526k with an unvested pipeline and a $240k base\u2014this initial package does not yet create a compelling enough financial case to make the jump.*\n>\n> *If we can get closer to the following numbers, I would be thrilled to sign immediately:*\n> * **Base Salary:** *$255,000 (to reflect my current base and stay aligned with the band)*\n> * **Equity:** *$1,280,000 over 4 years*\n> * **Sign-on Bonus:** *$75,000 (to offset the unvested RSUs and transition risk)*\n>\n> *Is this something you can take back to the compensation committee?\"*\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n* **Do not accept** the first offer; it is only a ~5% increase on a 4-year annualized basis.\n* **Do not walk away**; Google expects you to negotiate, and they have significant budget headroom at L6 for base, equity, and sign-on.\n* If they meet you halfway (e.g., $245k\u2013$250k base, $1.2M equity, and a $50k\u2013$60k sign-on), you will have secured an extra **~$150k\u2013$200k in total value** over your tenure.",
