    "message": {
     "content": "### Short Answer: **Yes, you should seriously consider accepting it.**\n\nYour negotiation was successful: you pushed them from a **$552k/yr** package to **$608k/yr average** ($2.43M total value over 4 years). More importantly, because of how Google\u2019s vesting works, **they effectively solved your $200k unvested Meta RSU concern, even without an upfront sign-on bonus.**\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Real Year-by-Year Math\n\nGoogle typically front-loads initial RSU grants (standard **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** or **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%**). \n\nAssuming the standard 33% front-load, here is what your cash and liquid stock flow looks like compared to Meta:\n\n| Year | Meta (IC6 Baseline) | Google (Revised L6 Offer) | Net Annual Delta |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Year 1** | ~$476,000 \u2013 $526,000 | **~$710,400** <br> *(Base $240k + Bonus $48k + RSU $422.4k)* | **+$184,000 to +$234,000** |\n| **Year 2** | ~$526,000 | **~$710,400** <br> *(Base $240k + Bonus $48k + RSU $422.4k)* | **+$184,400** |\n| **Year 3** | ~$526,000 | **~$569,600** <br> *(Base $240k + Bonus $48k + RSU $281.6k)* | **+$43,600** *(+ Refreshes)* |\n| **Year 4** | ~$526,000 | **~$441,600** <br> *(Base $240k + Bonus $48k + RSU $153.6k)* | *Requires Refreshes to bridge* |\n| **4-Yr Total**| **~$2,104,000** | **~$2,432,000** | **+$328,000 overall** |\n\n---\n\n### 2. Why Missing the Sign-On Bonus Isn't a Dealbreaker\n\nWhen you asked for compensation to cover the **$200,000** you were leaving on the table at Meta, Google responded by increasing your total equity grant by **$200,000** (from $1.08M to $1.28M).\n\n* **In Year 1 alone**, you will vest **$422,400** of Google stock.\n* Even after subtracting the $200,000 you leave behind at Meta, your Year 1 equity payout will be **higher than your Meta annual equity run rate ($250k)** by over **$170,000**.\n* You are not \"losing\" money during the transition; your first-year liquid income will surge past $700,000.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Offer Calibration\n\n* **$1.28M equity** is near the top of the band for standard L6 PM offers without competing written counteroffers.\n* **$240k base** matches your current rate and sits comfortably in the upper half of their $183k\u2013$271k band.\n* When Google says *\"this is our best offer\"* after bumping equity by $200k and matching your base, continuing to push for a sign-on bonus will yield diminishing returns and could cause unnecessary friction.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What Should Drive Your Decision Now\n\nSince the compensation is mathematically superior for Years 1\u20133, your decision comes down to qualitative factors:\n\n1. **Product Scope & Impact:** Do you find the product area, team charter, and problem space at Google more compelling than your current Meta scope?\n2. **Career Velocity (L7 / Director Track):** Meta\u2019s culture is generally faster-paced with clearer ownership lines, but can have higher volatility. Google offers broader scale and stability, though navigating cross-functional consensus and promotions (L6 to L7) can take longer.\n3. **Burnout vs. WLB:** If you want a change of pace or better work-life balance, Google typically provides a more sustainable rhythm than Meta.\n\n---\n\n### Recommendation\n\n**Accept the offer.** You successfully extracted an additional **$200,000 in equity and raised your base**, putting you over **$700k in Year 1 total comp**. If the team and product alignment feel right, this is a strong financial and career move.",
